WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.200
At a quarter to two in the morning, three graphics cards read one hundred percent busy.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.000
They had read that the whole evening.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.400
And since eighteen twenty-one, seven and a half hours before, not one of them had finished a single segment of work.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.200
Here is why.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.000
Each hour, the jobs the fleet finished.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.800
From eighteen forty-two the Mac closed its own connections to the fleet, a hundred and twenty-three times.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:39.800
Every job on a closed connection went back on the queue and was started again, two hundred and seventy-six times.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.200
The first copy was never stopped.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.200
The fleet went from seventy-nine jobs an hour to twenty-four.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.000
This is the fleet's Timeline for that day.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Blue and green are work.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.400
The orange-red wall is jobs that failed or were sent back.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.400
It begins at eighteen forty, on every row at once.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.200
What closed the connections is not found.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:12.800
This film is about the fault that turned a nuisance into seven lost hours, and what was built after.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.600
The Orange Queue.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.600
One night on the fleet, the fourteenth of November, told from the keeper's own events.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.800
Begin from far away.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.400
There are three territories.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.800
The Mac is the fleet's coordinator:

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.400
one process, the keeper, owns the queue, and holds one connection to each machine.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.000
Below it, the machines.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.800
They run the jobs on their cores, and each job's ending travels back up the same connection.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.200
Beside them, three graphics cards, an island of nets on each.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.200
A segment of an island's work rides that connection too, and ends the same way.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.000
Everything that follows hangs on that connection.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:06.600
Between the Mac and the rest there is one border, and one way over it.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.800
Going out, a job is one ssh command, open until the job ends.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.200
Coming back, the job's exit code.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.400
Or, when the connection itself fails, two fifty-five.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.200
And a card's segment needs thirty-one to forty-five minutes on one connection.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.600
That night, no connection lasted seven.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.800
First, an ordinary day.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:29.600
Five arrows:

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.800
how a job runs, and what it rides.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.000
Start on the Mac.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.800
One process, the keeper, owns the queue.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.600
Each machine has a lane or two.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:51.000
A lane takes a job from the queue, runs it on its machine, and files it as done or as failed.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.400
A job runs as one ssh command, open until the job ends.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:06.800
Since the fourth of November every command to a machine rides one connection, held by a small process on the Mac:

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.600
the master.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:17.800
Over that connection the command reaches the machine, and the job starts in a process group of its own.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.800
When it ends, its exit code comes back as ssh's.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.200
On an ordinary day seventy-nine jobs an hour finish this way.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.200
A card's work is a segment:

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.000
a hundred generations of two thousand and forty-eight nets, on one card.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.200
It needs thirty-one to forty-five minutes, and all of it on one connection.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:50.600
A segment loads the island's saved state when it starts, and writes it back when it ends.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.600
It also imports the champions of the other two islands.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.800
Remember that file.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.600
Now the night.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:00.400
Seven arrows.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.800
The fault is in the third.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.400
At eighteen forty-two the Mac begins closing its own masters:

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.600
a hundred and twenty-three times by one thirty-six.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.800
The closes keep time with the five-minute housekeeping, two minutes after it begins.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.400
The obvious mechanism was tested, and is not it.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.400
The cause is not established.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.800
When a master closes, every job on that machine ends at once, each with ssh's own failure:

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:33.600
two fifty-five.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.400
The keeper reads that as the connection's fault, and not the job's.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:48.200
So the lane puts the job back on the queue, three times at most, and starts it again.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.000
Two hundred and seventy-six times that night.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
And here is the fault.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.400
It did not stop the copy already running on the machine.

00:04:60.000 --> 00:05:03.200
A closed connection does not stop the job.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.000
Each drop left one more copy running.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.400
By one fifty-five columbia ran fifteen census jobs on eight cores, fourteen of them left behind.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.200
Its load was a hundred and twelve.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.600
On the cards it was the same.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:29.800
By one fifty-five boburner's one card ran six crucible processes at once, and only one was a segment the keeper was waiting for.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.600
The cards read a hundred percent busy, on work already written off.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.800
Every copy writes the island's state when it ends.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:48.400
A copy that began from an older state can write over a newer one, and the island goes backwards.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.800
Whether that happened was not measured.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.800
One failed segment fails the five or six queued behind it.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:07.400
Then the card's lane stood idle about thirty-one minutes, until the planner asked a fresh chain, and lost that one to the next drop.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:13.000
On three cards, in seven and a half hours, not one segment was done.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.000
And what was built after, that night and the next morning.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:20.800
Four arrows.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.600
The first change.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:31.000
On two fifty-five, the lane stops the copy on the machine before the job goes back.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:37.800
A machine that cannot be told is written down, and its copy is stopped when it answers.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.800
The check closes a real connection under a real job.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.000
Two writers before.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:43.800
One after.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.600
The second change.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.600
A segment holds its island's lock for its whole life.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.600
A newcomer that finds the lock held is the one the keeper is waiting for.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.800
It stops the holder, and takes the lock.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.400
One island, one writer.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:05.800
The third.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.200
Nothing read a temperature before this.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:18.600
Now, each two minutes, the keeper reads every machine's sensors and its card, over a connection of its own, and never the one the jobs ride.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:29.200
A machine at its limit finishes the job in hand and takes nothing new, until it reads eight degrees under.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:36.800
And after any drop, every lane of that machine rests sixty seconds, and the machine is read at once.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.000
No real machine has yet reached a limit.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.400
What is proved, and what is not.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.000
A real connection was closed under a real job:

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.800
two writers before the change, one after.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.600
The lock was proved on calber, with sleep standing for a segment.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.400
The heat hold was proved against a file standing for a sensor.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.200
Not proved:

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:09.800
no real machine has reached a limit, and no segment carrying the lock had run on a real island.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.000
The islands' states were not read for damage.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.800
And the cause of the closes is as open as it was.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.400
A job put back is stopped where it ran, first.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.000
One island, one writer.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.000
A drop is followed by a rest and a reading.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.400
These make a close cost minutes.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.800
They do not stop the closes.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.800
What closed the connections that night is still not found.
